  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Where was the Inca capital?",
      "a": "Cusco, high in the Andes of Peru. The Inca called their empire Tawantinsuyu, meaning the four parts together, and Cusco was where the four parts met.",
      "asks": [
        "What was Cusco?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How long were the Inca roads?",
      "a": "The road network, the Qhapaq Nan, was around 40,000 kilometres long, reaching from today's Colombia and Ecuador to Chile and Argentina. Parts are now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
      "asks": [
        "How big was the Inca road system?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who were the chasquis?",
      "a": "Relay runners who carried messages and small items along the roads. Each ran a few kilometres to the next rest house and passed the message on, so news moved very fast.",
      "asks": [
        "How did Inca messengers work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a quipu?",
      "a": "A set of coloured cotton or wool strings with knots. Knots stood for numbers in ones, tens, hundreds and so on, so officials could record taxes, people and goods. Some experts think quipus may have recorded stories too.",
      "asks": [
        "How did the Inca keep records?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why did the Inca build storehouses?",
      "a": "To keep food, cloth and weapons for armies, workers and times of famine. Thousands of storehouses, called qollqa, stood along the roads and near cities.",
      "asks": [
        "What were qollqa?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Inca road system, the Qhapaq Nan, became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2014.",
    "Machu Picchu was built around 1450, probably for the Inca ruler Pachacuti.",
    "Quechua, the language of the Inca, is spoken by millions of people in the Andes today."
  ],
